":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zunlei","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yijie","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Mingli","family":"Song","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xinyu","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Chun","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2023,11,9]],"date-time":"2023-11-09T15:41:27Z","timestamp":1699544487000},"score":14.694801,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=4445226"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4445226","published":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,31]],"date-time":"2026-07-31T03:01:42Z","timestamp":1785466902180,"version":"3.56.0"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]],"date-time":"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1767225600000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/www.uspto.gov\/ip-policy\/copyright-policy\/copyright-basics"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large-codebook vector-quantized (VQ) image models can lose effective capacity when encoder drift outpaces sparse codebook updates. We provide controlled evidence that drift amplifies codebook lag, assignment churn, quantization error, and collapse. Based on this observation, we propose DriftVQ, a visual-tokenizer training strategy combining a local drift-tracking embedding loss, Stage-1 code replacement, and a freeze\u2013warm-up\u2013refine schedule. DriftVQ retains hard nearest-neighbor quantization while using auxiliary soft weights only to update nearby non-winning codes during encoder non-stationarity. After commitment loss plateaus, the encoder is frozen, the quantizer and decoder are stabilized without adversarial pressure, and adversarial refinement resumes under fixed latent geometry. On ImageNet-1k at 128 \u00d7 128 with 65,536 codes, DriftVQ achieves 100% utilization and reduces reconstruction FID from 2.39 to 2.10 relative to SimVQ. Ablations confirm distinct contributions from each component. DriftVQ also improves 256 \u00d7 256 reconstruction and downstream latent-diffusion generation.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7208569","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,31]],"date-time":"2026-07-31T02:44:43Z","timestamp":1785465883000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["DriftVQ: Stable Large-Codebook Visual Tokenization through Encoder-Drift-AwareTraining"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"given":"Hao","family":"Lu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yongxin","family":"Guo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Onur  Can.","family":"Koyun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhengjie","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Abbas","family":"Alili","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Metin  Nafi","family":"Gurcan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,31]],"date-time":"2026-07-31T02:44:43Z","timestamp":1785465883000},"score":14.634985,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=7208569"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7208569","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]],"date-time":"2025-01-21T05:11:01Z","timestamp":1737436261989,"version":"3.33.0"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,20]]},"group-title":"display","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Copernicus GmbH","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Under the background of global warming, sea ice changes rapidly. Sea ice drift is an important indicator for sea ice flux, atmospheric and ocean circulation, and ship navigation. Currently, the large-scale observed sea ice drift datasets are mainly obtained based on single-sensor remotely sensed data, which suffer low spatial resolution or poor spatial continuity. Considering that passive microwave radiometer and medium-resolution optical sensor complement each other in terms of spatial resolution and continuity, this study proposed a novel sea ice drift retrieval method based on Fengyun-3D (FY-3D) multi-sensor data. The proposed method is summarized as follows. First, low resolution sea ice drift fields were obtained from FY-3D Microwave Radiation Imager (MWRI) data based on the normalized cross-correlation pattern-matching method. Then, fine resolution vectors were extracted from FY-3D Medium-Resolution Spectral Imager (MERSI) data based on A-KAZE feature-tracking method. Finally, the low resolution pattern-matching vectors and fine resolution feature-tracking vectors were merged together based on Co-Kriging algorithm to obtain the final sea ice drift result. The proposed method was evaluated by comparing the buoy displacements obtained from the International Arctic Buoy Program (IABP) with the retrieved merged vectors from FY-3D remotely sensed images collected in the Beaufort Sea, the East Siberian Sea, and the Fram Strait on 2020. The results showed that the proposed method can retrieve accurate, fine resolution and spatial continuous sea ice motion fields.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.5194\/egusphere-egu24-13914","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,9]],"date-time":"2024-03-09T00:35:18Z","timestamp":1709944518000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Sea Ice Drift Retrieval based on Fengyun-3D Multi-Sensor Data"],"prefix":"10.5194","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6639-3092","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xue","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhuoqi","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhizhuo","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ruirui","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ran","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Fengming","family":"Hui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xiao","family":"Cheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"3145","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,20]],"date-time":"2025-01-20T11:49:55Z","timestamp":1737373795000},"score":14.477579,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/meetingorganizer.copernicus.org\/EGU24\/EGU24-13914.html"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,20]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5194\/egusphere-egu24-13914","published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,20]]},"subtype":"other"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,14]],"date-time":"2026-08-14T11:29:49Z","timestamp":1786706989209,"version":"build-2736575974"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2021]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Background: Based on clinical observation, inguinal hernia sac, extended tissue from peritoneum, gradually enlarged in size with hernia disease time and prolapsed tissue volume. The sac is often respected during hernia surgery procedures and then be discarded as a medical waste. We hypothesize Mesenchymal stem cells present in the hernia sac tissue development. Mesenchymal stem cells (MSCs) are multipotent stromal cells that can mature or differentiate into a variety of cell types. Thus, the current study aimed to testify the hypothesis that hernia sac contains MSCs, and thus might be a suitable source to harvest mesenchymal stem cell during inguinal hernia surgery. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Methods: Between July 2019 and June 2020, four hernia sacs were resected during hernia surgery. The fresh hernia sacs were then sent to the laboratory within 48 h for mesenchymal extraction. Mesenchymal tissue was then obtained using the Miltenyi gentleMACS Dissociator. The presence of MSC was determined by employing the minimum criteria for defining multipotent MSCs. Markers CD105, CD73, and CD90 were used to assess expressions \u226595%, while markers CD45, CD34, CD11b, CD19, and HLA-DR were used to assess for their lack of expression (\u22642%) in order to determine the presence of MSCs in the hernia sac. Moreover, van Kosa staining, Alcian blue staining, and Oil Red O staining were used to verify the cells\u2019 ability for differentiation. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Results: Cells retrieved from the hernia sacs displayed a spindle shape morphology and exhibited adherence to plastics. The cells surface immunophenotypic profile was confirmed using surface markers APC-A (CD73), FITC-A (CD90) and PerCP-Cy5-5-A (CD105), with results showing 100%, 100%, and 99.2% respectively, strongly indicating the presence of MSCs. Moreover, staining of in vitro cell cultures showed in vitro differentiation of precursor cells into osteoblasts, adipocytes, and chondroblasts, suggesting positive differentiation ability and identification of MSCs. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Conclusion: The hernia sac is a novel source of MSCs that can be easily obtained and stored for future usage.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.3882706","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2021,7,12]],"date-time":"2021-07-12T23:05:10Z","timestamp":1626131110000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["The Hernia Sac - A Suitable Source for Obtaining Mesenchymal Stem Cells"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3838-5949","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Alpha Dian-Yu","family":"Lin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4136-7613","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Min-Che","family":"Tung","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chin-Heng","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,14]],"date-time":"2026-08-14T10:42:11Z","timestamp":1786704131000},"score":14.244339,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=3882706"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.3882706","published":{"date-parts":[[2021]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"institution":[{"name":"bioRxiv"}],"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,20]],"date-time":"2026-06-20T04:51:17Z","timestamp":1781931077979,"version":"3.54.5"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,18]]},"reference-count":13,"publisher":"openRxiv","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,18]],"date-time":"2026-06-18T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1781740800000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/www.biorxiv.org\/about\/FAQ#license"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"accepted":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,18]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>Senescence human bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells (BMSCs), vulnerable to age-related defects, is poor in tissue regeneration. Cells in bone marrow accumulated senescent contributing to the development of metabolic energy regulation hold prospects for therapeutic advances. This study aimed to evaluate energy metabolic changes in male bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells senescence process. Our research established cell specific surface marker and enzymes expression level changes, as well as ECAR and OCR resonance. Notably, CD14, HLA-DRB1 and CD90 upregulated, glycolysis-related genes are increased, tricarboxylic acid cycle-related genes are decreased. We firstly identified links between time-dependent cell aging process and energy metabolism in BMSCs.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.64898\/2026.06.17.732798","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,18]],"date-time":"2026-06-18T12:30:13Z","timestamp":1781785813000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Energy Metabolizes in Male Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cells Aging Process"],"prefix":"10.64898","author":[{"given":"Yue","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Life Science and Technology, Harbin Institute of Technology, Harbin 150080, Heilongjiang, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Huiyu","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Pharmacy, Qiqihar Medical University, Qiqihar 161000, Heilongjiang, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xin","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Life Science and Technology, Harbin Institute of Technology, Harbin 150080, Heilongjiang, China"},{"name":"School of Pharmacy, Qiqihar Medical University, Qiqihar 